A||||21||hyhy4u||Engine Running....cool???||||1||
Z||000000||hyhy4u||07-08-2001||12:24 AM||||Just by reading some other posts on here, it seems my car runs cooler then everyone elses, is this true or am i just nuts...my car after only about 2 min of running goes to about 120 degrees then stays put there for....ever pertty much..unless i get stuck in a traffic jam then it goes to like 140 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> , lol...what do ya guys think??, im guessing it cant really be bad can it?..||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||07-08-2001||12:50 AM||||How can you tell that your car is at 120?  The lowest the gauge reads is at 160, and my car is normally just below the first mark, which is around 180...||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||07-08-2001||12:52 AM||||Yep, the gauge is 120 degrees from the bottom to the middle (100-220) and 60 degrees from the middle to the top (220-280). Definitely not a precision meter. Now the oil pressure gauge is amazingly accurate.<P>I scanned my ECM and added a series resistor to the temp gauge so it reads 220 when the ECM sensor sees 220. <P>Edited for clarity.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jncomutt||07-08-2001||12:54 AM||||Well, while I don't have technical data about whats really goin on, I had the similar problem.  My car would only get to 1/4 of the gauge.  Maybe in hot day traffic, it went to the half way mark.  Turns out that the thermostat was stuck.(open?)  Either way, I replaced it and found it to be running at a much more regular temp.  Then decided to switch to the low temp thermostat, but you get the idea...||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||07-08-2001||02:35 AM||||1/4 scale is roughly 180-195-ish.||||reg||1
Z||000005||SteveJ||07-08-2001||07:24 AM||||Run your car until it should reach normal temp then put your hand on the radiator hose at the engine. It should be so hot you can't hold your hand there which would be about 195 degrees. If you can hold your hand on it then the thermostat is stuck open and needs replacement. Manufactures of thermostats would rather have their product fail in the open condition rather than closed for obvious reasons. Now if this turns out to be the case you will notice a nice jump in power and drivability with the new thermostat. But if the hose is hot as hell when you touch it the gauge just isn't accurate, which to me is a "so what" situation.||||reg||1
Z||000006||hyhy4u||07-08-2001||02:09 PM||||My car dosnt have a theromostat in it, lol...when i got the engine work done on it i know they replaces the fan relay and hes like..theres no thermostat in it.if you want you can throw one in but i probobly wont need it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Hartz||07-08-2001||02:27 PM||||You should use some kind of thermostat.  It provides enough "resistance" in the cooling system to prevent the coolant from circulating too fast.  If the coolant flows too quickly, it won't have the desired cooling effect.  Also, having a thermostat will allow your car to reach "closed loop" operation quicker.<P>Just my $.02<BR><P>------------------<BR>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<P> <IMG SRC="http://home.mindspring.com/~hartzfeld/_uimages/signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||hyhy4u||07-08-2001||03:58 PM||||My temp gauge starts at 100 not 160 or anything..||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroJoe||07-08-2001||06:08 PM||||yeah, that's an oops on me, I must have been thinking of some other car's gauge, I realized that it started out at 100 today.||||reg||1
Z||000010||SteveJ||07-09-2001||10:11 AM||||Any mechanic that tells you that you don't need a thermostat isn't much of a mechanic. You <u><B>do</B></u> need one and it must work. Without one your engine is <u><B><FONT COLOR="#=0000ff">cold</FONT></B> all the time</u> and will not develop all its power and will have drivability problems. Gas mileage will be very poor also. I just can't imagine any reputable mechanic not putting one in. You need to get the one recommended for the engine and put it in. . .and don't get one of those 160 degree ones because your engine will still be too cold with that.<p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 07-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Jncomutt||07-09-2001||11:51 AM||||SteveJ, thanx I was just about to get one of those 160 from the fierostore this week.  Moving down to the 180 I found at the local Pep Boys should be fine I guess...||||reg||1
Z||000012||hyhy4u||07-09-2001||01:11 PM||||Okie dokie..il run down to Autozone later today and pick one up and throw it in..Thanks Guys!<P> Jason.||||reg||1
Z||000013||My7Fieros||07-09-2001||04:35 PM||||<B>Whoa! </B> Your car ISNT running that cool...your temp sending unit has crapped out. I know, I had the same prob on my Formula. <BR>Pop the decklid and look on the rear side of the engine (trunk side) on the drivers side...on the lower corner of the valve cover youll see the temp sending unit. Replace it and you see your temp guage work properly. The part runs around 20 bucks (like most sensors for the Fiero) at autozone. Thats your prob, car isnt really running that cool. Try it, you'll see. Good luck, M7F||||reg||4
Z||000014||hyhy4u||07-10-2001||01:48 PM||||Can that really be the case cause like i said the temp goes up when im stuck in traffic and stuff so if it crapped out wouldnt it not do this...ohh..and i did finally get the blasted radiator cap off...i had to use a adjustable oil filter wrench to get it turned...actually worked really really good, lol..but there was a thermostat in it..so now what??||||reg||1
